<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Molossians</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Molossians were the leading tribe of ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epirus">Epirus</a>, in north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dodona">Dodona</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ioannina">Ioannina</a> basin. Ruled by the Aeacid kings, who claimed descent from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achilles">Achilles</a>, they united the Epirote tribes under their leadership and produced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olympia">Olympias</a> and Pyrrhus. In 167 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> sold 150,000 Epirotes into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>, devastating their land.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/molossians.jpg" alt="Molossians" width="320" height="305" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Megistias · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Epirus_antiquus_tabula.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Molossians were a group of ancient Greek tribes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epirus">Epirus</a>. Together with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chaonians">Chaonians</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thesprotians">Thesprotians</a> they formed the main tribal groupings of the northwestern Greek group, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strabo">Strabo</a> counted them among the foremost of the fourteen tribes of Epirus. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/late-bronze-age">Late Bronze Age</a> they were probably located over much of the central and western ranges of the Pindos, and the area of Pogoni, with its many tumuli burials, has been regarded as their heartland.</p><p>They first lived in small unwalled settlements scattered through river valleys and lakeside areas. Their expansion in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epirus">Epirus</a> possibly began in the early 6th century BCE, mainly at the expense of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thesprotians">Thesprotians</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Around 370 BCE the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aeacidae">Aeacid dynasty</a> created the first centralised state in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epirus">Epirus</a>. In 359 BCE the Molossian princess <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olympia">Olympias</a> married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-macedon">Philip II of Macedon</a> and became the mother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a>. Her brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-i-of-epirus">Alexander the Molossian</a> led an expedition to southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and was defeated at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-pandosia">Battle of Pandosia</a> in 331 BCE. After his death in 330 BCE the name Epirus appears for the first time as a single political unit, under the Molossian dynasty. Pyrrhus came to the throne in 295 BCE and defeated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> at the Battle of Heraclea in 280 BCE, but his costly victory at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ascoli-piceno">Asculum</a> in 279 BCE gave the term Pyrrhic victory its meaning.</p><p>In 233 BCE Deidamia, the last of the Aeacid house, was murdered and a federal republic, the Epirote League, was set up. The Epirotes sided with Macedonia in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-macedonian-war">Third Macedonian War</a> (171-168 BCE) and, after the Roman victory, 150,000 of them, mostly Molossians, were enslaved and sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> by decision of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-senate">Roman Senate</a>. Some recent research treats the figures of destruction as symbolic, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greek-language">Greek language</a> remained vital in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epirus">Epirus</a> in later centuries.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The Molossians spoke a Northwest Doric dialect of Greek and worshipped the Greek gods, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeus">Zeus</a> supreme and the oracle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dodona">Dodona</a> in their land. Many <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> nevertheless regarded them as barbarians, because of their tribal organisation and pastoral economy. Their dynasty claimed descent from Molossus, a son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neoptolemus">Neoptolemus</a>, son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achilles">Achilles</a>, and scholars see this genealogy as an effort to heroise the house.</p><p>Around 370-368 BCE their officials were the king, the prostatai, a secretary, the hieromnemones and ten damiourgoi, one for each tribe. Each year the king sacrificed to Zeus Areios and swore to rule according to the laws. The Molossians were also known for their hounds, and their coins showed a dog on a shield.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Molossians” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Around 232 BCE the last of the royal Aeacid family, Deidamia, was killed and the <b>Molossians</b>, Chaonians and Thesprotians abolished the monarchy of the Kingdom of Epirus.</blockquote><a href="/state/epirote-league">Epirote League</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Molossians</b> gradually led the other Epirote tribes, the Chaonians and Thesprotians, in an alliance.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-epirus">Kingdom of Epirus</a></li></ul></section>
